1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:03,560
Chicago. January 13, 1879.

2
00:00:04,225 --> 00:00:06,535
Inside a hotel called the Palmer House,

3
00:00:06,925 --> 00:00:09,615
a United States Army court opens its doors.

4
00:00:10,264 --> 00:00:12,614
And the man on trial asked to be here.

5
00:00:13,144 --> 00:00:14,074
Major Marcus Reno.

6
00:00:14,912 --> 00:00:16,182
For two and a half years,

7
00:00:16,212 --> 00:00:17,722
America has called him a coward.

8
00:00:18,412 --> 00:00:21,132
A drunk. The officer who let George Armstrong

9
00:00:21,132 --> 00:00:22,302
Custer die.

10
00:00:22,842 --> 00:00:24,772
The accusation began with a book.

11
00:00:25,342 --> 00:00:26,412
Months after the battle,

12
00:00:26,502 --> 00:00:28,582
a writer named Frederick Whittaker published

13
00:00:28,582 --> 00:00:30,652
a heroic biography of Custer.

14
00:00:31,282 --> 00:00:34,012
In it, he blamed Custer's death on two officers:

15
00:00:34,542 --> 00:00:37,402
Major Reno and Captain Frederick Benteen.

16
00:00:37,402 --> 00:00:39,722
Whittaker demanded an official investigation.

17
00:00:39,722 --> 00:00:42,172
Reno decided to face it.

18
00:00:42,701 --> 00:00:44,431
Today, Reno wants a verdict.

19
00:00:44,881 --> 00:00:45,951
But to judge him,

20
00:00:46,041 --> 00:00:48,091
this court must answer a bigger question.

21
00:00:48,401 --> 00:00:50,401
Who really lost the Battle of the Little

22
00:00:50,401 --> 00:00:51,071
Bighorn?

23
00:00:51,717 --> 00:00:52,767
You've heard the legend.

24
00:00:52,917 --> 00:00:53,677
Now hear the evidence.

25
00:00:53,677 --> 00:00:55,807
And this time, you are the jury.

26
00:00:57,071 --> 00:00:58,891
The dead man at the center of this case

27
00:00:58,891 --> 00:01:02,191
is famous. George Armstrong Custer graduated

28
00:01:02,191 --> 00:01:04,161
last in his class at West Point.

29
00:01:04,811 --> 00:01:06,521
Two years later, at just 23,

30
00:01:07,011 --> 00:01:07,721
he was a general.

31
00:01:08,320 --> 00:01:10,750
Brave, reckless, and loved by the newspapers,

32
00:01:11,480 --> 00:01:13,970
he became the boy general of the Union cavalry.

33
00:01:14,653 --> 00:01:16,853
At Gettysburg, his Michigan cavalry helped

34
00:01:16,853 --> 00:01:18,853
stop a Confederate cavalry attack.

35
00:01:18,853 --> 00:01:21,833
At Appomattox, he was there when the war

36
00:01:21,833 --> 00:01:22,423
ended.

37
00:01:23,036 --> 00:01:25,166
But his record had dark marks too.

38
00:01:25,876 --> 00:01:28,816
In 1867, he was court-martialed for leaving

39
00:01:28,816 --> 00:01:30,296
his post to see his wife,

40
00:01:30,296 --> 00:01:32,186
and suspended for a year.

41
00:01:32,658 --> 00:01:35,478
And in 1868, he attacked a Cheyenne village

42
00:01:35,478 --> 00:01:36,888
on the Washita River.

43
00:01:37,538 --> 00:01:38,808
Women and children died.

44
00:01:39,218 --> 00:01:41,338
And Custer rode away without finding one

45
00:01:41,338 --> 00:01:42,268
of his own majors,

46
00:01:42,718 --> 00:01:44,068
Joel Elliott, and his men.

47
00:01:44,598 --> 00:01:45,928
They were later found dead.

48
00:01:46,546 --> 00:01:48,786
Elliott was a close friend of one of Custer's

49
00:01:48,786 --> 00:01:51,866
captains. His name was Frederick Benteen,

50
00:01:51,866 --> 00:01:53,416
and he never forgave Custer.

51
00:01:54,126 --> 00:01:55,906
Eight years later, at the Little Bighorn,

52
00:01:55,906 --> 00:01:58,306
Benteen would be the officer Custer called

53
00:01:58,306 --> 00:01:59,016
for help.

54
00:01:59,836 --> 00:02:01,506
Marcus Reno was his opposite.

55
00:02:02,176 --> 00:02:03,336
A quiet career officer,

56
00:02:03,336 --> 00:02:05,056
injured in the Civil War,

57
00:02:05,056 --> 00:02:07,396
with almost no experience fighting Plains

58
00:02:07,396 --> 00:02:10,806
warriors. In June 1876,

59
00:02:11,276 --> 00:02:13,296
he was Custer's second in command in the

60
00:02:13,296 --> 00:02:14,106
7th Cavalry.

61
00:02:15,012 --> 00:02:17,052
To understand how they got to that river,

62
00:02:17,052 --> 00:02:18,462
go back two years.

63
00:02:19,112 --> 00:02:22,192
In 1874, Custer led an expedition into the

64
00:02:22,192 --> 00:02:24,832
Black Hills, land promised to the Lakota

65
00:02:24,832 --> 00:02:27,162
by treaty. And he reported gold.

66
00:02:27,837 --> 00:02:30,577
Miners poured in. When the Lakota and their

67
00:02:30,577 --> 00:02:32,167
allies refused to sell,

68
00:02:32,457 --> 00:02:34,747
the army was sent to force them onto reservations.

69
00:02:35,885 --> 00:02:37,715
In the summer of 1876,

70
00:02:38,045 --> 00:02:39,935
three army columns closed in.

71
00:02:40,545 --> 00:02:42,375
The fastest was the 7th Cavalry.

72
00:02:43,045 --> 00:02:45,885
About 600 soldiers, plus scouts and civilians,

73
00:02:45,885 --> 00:02:47,035
under Custer.

74
00:02:47,841 --> 00:02:49,711
Eight days before Custer's attack,

75
00:02:50,141 --> 00:02:52,381
General George Crook's column ran into the

76
00:02:52,381 --> 00:02:54,061
same warriors at Rosebud Creek,

77
00:02:54,061 --> 00:02:57,191
and pulled back. Custer never got the news.

78
00:02:58,137 --> 00:03:00,917
Exhibit A. General Alfred Terry offered

79
00:03:00,917 --> 00:03:03,457
Custer a battery of Gatling guns and an

80
00:03:03,457 --> 00:03:04,967
extra battalion of cavalry.

81
00:03:05,777 --> 00:03:07,327
Custer turned both down.

82
00:03:07,986 --> 00:03:10,256
The prosecution says he threw away firepower.

83
00:03:11,106 --> 00:03:13,056
The defense says the guns were heavy,

84
00:03:13,086 --> 00:03:15,286
jammed often, and would have slowed a march

85
00:03:15,286 --> 00:03:17,196
of nearly 30 miles a day.

86
00:03:17,806 --> 00:03:18,756
Custer chose speed.

87
00:03:19,621 --> 00:03:22,921
Exhibit B. The army expected to face no

88
00:03:22,921 --> 00:03:24,631
more than 800 warriors,

89
00:03:24,921 --> 00:03:27,131
a number taken from reservation agents.

90
00:03:27,796 --> 00:03:30,176
In reality, the village on the Little Bighorn

91
00:03:30,176 --> 00:03:32,336
was one of the largest ever gathered on

92
00:03:32,336 --> 00:03:35,106
the northern plains. Thousands of people.

93
00:03:35,496 --> 00:03:38,166
Between 1,100 and 2,500 warriors.

94
00:03:39,276 --> 00:03:41,786
Exhibit C. Dawn, June 25.

95
00:03:42,376 --> 00:03:44,166
From a hilltop called the Crow's Nest,

96
00:03:44,476 --> 00:03:46,776
Custer's scouts spotted a massive pony herd,

97
00:03:46,776 --> 00:03:48,326
14 miles away.

98
00:03:48,842 --> 00:03:51,432
The scouts urged Custer to look for himself.

99
00:03:51,962 --> 00:03:52,912
Through the morning haze,

100
00:03:53,102 --> 00:03:54,332
he said he could see nothing.

101
00:03:55,021 --> 00:03:57,511
Custer wanted a day to scout before attacking.

102
00:03:58,121 --> 00:03:59,921
Then he learned his column had already been

103
00:03:59,921 --> 00:04:02,431
seen. Afraid the village would scatter,

104
00:04:02,761 --> 00:04:04,351
he decided to attack at once.

105
00:04:05,215 --> 00:04:07,605
Exhibit D. Custer split the regiment.

106
00:04:08,495 --> 00:04:10,315
Captain Frederick Benteen took three companies

107
00:04:10,315 --> 00:04:12,085
to scout the hills to the left.

108
00:04:12,875 --> 00:04:14,125
Reno took three companies,

109
00:04:14,315 --> 00:04:17,055
about 140 men, to charge the village from

110
00:04:17,055 --> 00:04:19,725
the south. Custer kept five companies,

111
00:04:19,915 --> 00:04:22,445
about 210 men, and rode north.

112
00:04:23,363 --> 00:04:25,693
The prosecution calls it a fatal mistake.

113
00:04:26,403 --> 00:04:28,883
He divided about 600 men in front of an

114
00:04:28,883 --> 00:04:30,273
enemy he had never counted.

115
00:04:31,043 --> 00:04:32,703
The defense says it was standard cavalry

116
00:04:32,703 --> 00:04:35,603
tactics. Custer had divided his command

117
00:04:35,603 --> 00:04:37,283
at the Washita too,

118
00:04:37,283 --> 00:04:38,433
and there it had worked.

119
00:04:39,200 --> 00:04:41,210
Reno crossed the river and charged.

120
00:04:41,920 --> 00:04:43,790
Then the warriors came out to meet him.

121
00:04:44,196 --> 00:04:46,706
He dismounted his men into a skirmish line.

122
00:04:47,416 --> 00:04:49,396
Outflanked, they fell back into the trees

123
00:04:49,396 --> 00:04:50,446
along the river.

124
00:04:50,907 --> 00:04:53,267
Beside Reno, his scout Bloody Knife was

125
00:04:53,267 --> 00:04:54,237
shot in the head.

126
00:04:54,947 --> 00:04:56,457
Reno ordered his men to mount,

127
00:04:56,607 --> 00:04:57,897
dismount, and mount again.

128
00:04:58,567 --> 00:05:00,407
Then he led a retreat that became a rout,

129
00:05:00,407 --> 00:05:02,837
back across the river and up the bluffs.

130
00:05:03,557 --> 00:05:06,257
At the inquiry, a civilian mule packer testified

131
00:05:06,257 --> 00:05:08,677
that Reno had been drinking that night on

132
00:05:08,677 --> 00:05:11,137
the hill. Other witnesses said they saw

133
00:05:11,137 --> 00:05:13,977
nothing wrong. The court had to decide who

134
00:05:13,977 --> 00:05:14,527
to believe.

135
00:05:15,406 --> 00:05:17,606
That retreat is the heart of the case against

136
00:05:17,606 --> 00:05:19,326
him. Did Reno panic?

137
00:05:19,326 --> 00:05:21,646
Or did he save his men from being wiped

138
00:05:21,646 --> 00:05:22,216
out?

139
00:05:22,929 --> 00:05:25,639
Exhibit E. As Custer neared the village,

140
00:05:25,729 --> 00:05:27,539
he finally saw how big it was.

141
00:05:28,149 --> 00:05:29,529
His adjutant scrawled a note,

142
00:05:29,529 --> 00:05:31,929
and trumpeter John Martin galloped off with

143
00:05:31,929 --> 00:05:32,219
it.

144
00:05:32,622 --> 00:05:34,472
“Benteen. Come on. Big village.

145
00:05:34,562 --> 00:05:35,612
Be quick. Bring packs.”

146
00:05:36,386 --> 00:05:38,706
It was the last message anyone ever received

147
00:05:38,706 --> 00:05:41,256
from Custer. Benteen did come.

148
00:05:41,586 --> 00:05:43,296
But he joined Reno on the hill.

149
00:05:44,126 --> 00:05:45,976
Why didn't Benteen ride to Custer?

150
00:05:46,466 --> 00:05:48,206
He later said Reno's men were shattered,

151
00:05:48,206 --> 00:05:49,656
and he could not leave them.

152
00:05:50,186 --> 00:05:52,016
They also waited for the pack train,

153
00:05:52,286 --> 00:05:54,296
the mules carrying the reserve ammunition.

154
00:05:54,986 --> 00:05:56,216
More than an hour passed.

155
00:05:57,252 --> 00:05:59,482
Now, the witnesses this court never called.

156
00:06:00,132 --> 00:06:02,632
The Lakota, Cheyenne and Arapaho who were

157
00:06:02,632 --> 00:06:03,422
in that village.

158
00:06:03,926 --> 00:06:06,346
Decades later, many of them told their stories

159
00:06:06,346 --> 00:06:08,196
to interviewers and historians.

160
00:06:08,946 --> 00:06:10,866
And their accounts changed how the battle

161
00:06:10,866 --> 00:06:11,676
is understood.

162
00:06:12,513 --> 00:06:14,533
Weeks earlier, at a Sun Dance,

163
00:06:14,533 --> 00:06:16,493
the Lakota leader Sitting Bull had seen

164
00:06:16,493 --> 00:06:19,343
a vision: soldiers falling into their camp.

165
00:06:19,931 --> 00:06:23,071
On June 25, war leaders like Gall and Crazy

166
00:06:23,071 --> 00:06:24,461
Horse led the defense.

167
00:06:25,351 --> 00:06:27,821
No confirmed photograph of Crazy Horse exists.

168
00:06:28,604 --> 00:06:30,634
To them, this wasn't a last stand.

169
00:06:31,164 --> 00:06:32,754
It was the defense of their families.

170
00:06:33,656 --> 00:06:36,676
Lakota and Cheyenne accounts describe Custer's

171
00:06:36,676 --> 00:06:38,776
battalion breaking apart on the ridges,

172
00:06:38,776 --> 00:06:40,886
and a fight that was over fast.

173
00:06:41,512 --> 00:06:44,732
In 1983, a grass fire burned across the

174
00:06:44,732 --> 00:06:47,552
battlefield. Archaeologists walked the bare

175
00:06:47,552 --> 00:06:49,712
ground and mapped thousands of bullets and

176
00:06:49,712 --> 00:06:52,612
cartridge cases. They showed that many warriors

177
00:06:52,612 --> 00:06:55,432
carried repeating rifles, and that Custer's

178
00:06:55,432 --> 00:06:57,702
companies collapsed one after another,

179
00:06:57,772 --> 00:06:59,862
not in one heroic last circle.

180
00:07:00,581 --> 00:07:03,641
Every one of the roughly 210 men with Custer

181
00:07:03,641 --> 00:07:05,391
was killed. In all,

182
00:07:05,721 --> 00:07:08,571
268 men of his command died.

183
00:07:09,064 --> 00:07:11,154
Among the dead were two of Custer's brothers,

184
00:07:11,584 --> 00:07:13,743
his nephew, and his brother-in-law.

185
00:07:14,453 --> 00:07:15,803
Two days after the battle,

186
00:07:16,153 --> 00:07:18,473
soldiers on Custer's field found a badly

187
00:07:18,473 --> 00:07:20,423
wounded horse named Comanche.

188
00:07:20,713 --> 00:07:22,683
He became a symbol of the disaster.

189
00:07:23,513 --> 00:07:25,693
The 7th Cavalry ordered that he never be

190
00:07:25,693 --> 00:07:26,383
ridden again.

191
00:07:27,259 --> 00:07:29,859
On Reno's hill, officers heard firing to

192
00:07:29,859 --> 00:07:32,619
the north. Captain Thomas Weir rode toward

193
00:07:32,619 --> 00:07:35,149
it without orders. From a high point,

194
00:07:35,219 --> 00:07:36,439
they saw dust and smoke,

195
00:07:36,439 --> 00:07:38,529
and warriors turning toward them.

196
00:07:38,940 --> 00:07:40,410
They fell back, dug in,

197
00:07:40,480 --> 00:07:42,230
and held on through another day,

198
00:07:42,380 --> 00:07:45,363
until relief arrived on June 27.

199
00:07:45,593 --> 00:07:47,933
Without water, the wounded begged for a

200
00:07:47,933 --> 00:07:50,793
drink. Volunteers crawled down to the river

201
00:07:50,793 --> 00:07:52,423
under fire to bring it back.

202
00:07:53,133 --> 00:07:54,793
Several of them later received the Medal

203
00:07:54,793 --> 00:07:55,463
of Honor.

204
00:07:56,429 --> 00:07:59,539
The verdict. After 26 days of testimony,

205
00:07:59,889 --> 00:08:01,969
the court found that Reno's conduct did

206
00:08:01,969 --> 00:08:03,459
not deserve its censure.

207
00:08:03,828 --> 00:08:05,298
Its words were faint praise.

208
00:08:06,108 --> 00:08:07,248
Some of Reno's officers,

209
00:08:07,248 --> 00:08:09,708
the court said, had shown more courage than

210
00:08:09,708 --> 00:08:12,008
he did. But there was nothing in his conduct

211
00:08:12,008 --> 00:08:14,038
that required the court's censure.

212
00:08:14,654 --> 00:08:17,014
The Army's top legal officer wrote that

213
00:08:17,014 --> 00:08:19,614
the accusation that Custer died because

214
00:08:19,614 --> 00:08:21,104
of Reno's cowardice was,

215
00:08:21,534 --> 00:08:22,784
quote, “set to rest.”

216
00:08:23,723 --> 00:08:25,533
It wasn't. Years later,

217
00:08:25,703 --> 00:08:27,503
enlisted men said they had been pressured

218
00:08:27,503 --> 00:08:30,723
to protect Reno. One officer admitted there

219
00:08:30,723 --> 00:08:33,013
was a private understanding to save him.

220
00:08:33,540 --> 00:08:35,820
Soon after, Reno was court-martialed for

221
00:08:35,820 --> 00:08:38,660
unrelated misconduct and thrown out of the

222
00:08:38,660 --> 00:08:41,030
army. He died in 1889,

223
00:08:41,680 --> 00:08:42,570
still in disgrace.

224
00:08:43,536 --> 00:08:46,086
And one person never stopped defending Custer.

225
00:08:46,796 --> 00:08:49,016
His widow, Elizabeth, spent the next 57

226
00:08:49,016 --> 00:08:50,766
years protecting his name.

227
00:08:51,336 --> 00:08:52,336
She wrote three books,

228
00:08:52,336 --> 00:08:54,856
gave lectures, and outlived almost every

229
00:08:54,856 --> 00:08:56,886
officer who could have criticized him.

230
00:08:57,655 --> 00:09:01,635
Then, in 1967, a military review board reversed

231
00:09:01,635 --> 00:09:04,955
his dismissal. Marcus Reno was reburied

232
00:09:04,955 --> 00:09:06,735
with honors, on the battlefield,

233
00:09:06,735 --> 00:09:09,345
in the cemetery named after Custer.

234
00:09:10,284 --> 00:09:12,254
So who lost the Little Bighorn?

235
00:09:12,825 --> 00:09:14,805
Custer, who split his command against an

236
00:09:14,805 --> 00:09:16,115
enemy he never counted?

237
00:09:16,825 --> 00:09:19,305
Reno, who retreated? Benteen,

238
00:09:19,305 --> 00:09:20,275
who didn't come quick?

239
00:09:20,665 --> 00:09:23,005
Or the men who sent soldiers into land promised

240
00:09:23,005 --> 00:09:23,815
by treaty?

241
00:09:24,688 --> 00:09:26,558
Or maybe no one lost it at all.

242
00:09:26,948 --> 00:09:29,398
Maybe the Lakota and Cheyenne simply won.

243
00:09:30,168 --> 00:09:32,088
Within a year, the army returned in force,

244
00:09:32,088 --> 00:09:34,118
and the Black Hills were taken anyway.

245
00:09:35,009 --> 00:09:37,989
In 1991, the battlefield's name was changed,

246
00:09:37,989 --> 00:09:41,229
from Custer Battlefield to Little Bighorn

247
00:09:41,229 --> 00:09:44,279
Battlefield National Monument. Today,

248
00:09:44,549 --> 00:09:46,649
a memorial to the Native warriors stands

249
00:09:46,649 --> 00:09:48,179
near the soldiers' monument.

250
00:09:49,206 --> 00:09:50,256
You've heard the evidence.

251
00:09:50,906 --> 00:09:51,616
You are the jury.

252
00:09:52,206 --> 00:09:54,106
Who's guilty? Tell us in the comments,

253
00:09:54,106 --> 00:09:56,606
and subscribe to Buried Chronicles for more

254
00:09:56,606 --> 00:09:58,356
of history's hidden turning points.

